## Objective

Implement the deliberately minimal macOS and Windows process backend and release-preparation smoke required to prove packaged candidate binaries can be launched, identified, bounded, and cleaned up.

This is not a semantic, compiler, provider, Test Explorer, DAP, harness, oracle, framework, or performance parity program. Linux remains authoritative for those areas.

## Target scope

For each intended macOS/Windows release target:

- obtain the exact candidate archive/artifact from the release matrix;
- verify artifact hash, target, version, and subject identity;
- extract/install into an isolated prefix;
- launch packaged `perllsp` and, where included, `perl-dap` for bounded identity/help or initialize/shutdown smoke;
- capture bounded stdout/stderr and exit status;
- enforce deadline and terminate/clean the started process;
- prove no workspace-built binary or host PATH substitution executed;
- emit target-specific process/install evidence.

## Process backend subset

Implement only what release prep requires:

- direct structured spawn;
- cwd and explicit environment;
- bounded stdout/stderr capture;
- deadline/timeout;
- terminate/kill of the process started by the smoke;
- terminal result and cleanup state;
- executable/artifact identity;
- public redaction.

Full descendant-tree semantics, interactive debugger behavior, test runner behavior, long-lived streaming, and semantic environment discovery are not required unless the release smoke itself needs them.

## Candidate identity

Every smoke binds:

- candidate/release version;
- frozen swarm/release-repo SHA;
- target triple;
- archive and executable digest;
- extraction/install path role;
- VSIX managed-binary mapping where relevant;
- process plan/result ID;
- workflow run/attempt;
- platform runner image/version;
- claim boundary.

## Smoke matrix

### `perllsp`

At minimum:

- `--version` and/or reviewed identity command;
- `--help` bounded smoke;
- minimal initialize/shutdown transport smoke where current installed acceptance supports it;
- clean exit or explicit termination;
- no unexpected external-tool dependency.

### `perl-dap`

- version/help/launchability smoke only;
- DAP remains preview unless Linux installed evidence proves more;
- no required real debug session on macOS/Windows.

### VSIX

- install into isolated profile where infrastructure supports it;
- verify target selection resolves to the exact candidate binary;
- activation starts the candidate binary;
- protocol/binary identity is visible;
- uninstall/cleanup leaves no live candidate process.

## Negative controls

- wrong target artifact;
- stale/wrong version;
- missing executable;
- workspace binary earlier in PATH;
- archive hash mismatch;
- hung launch;
- process survives cleanup;
- VSIX maps to wrong target/binary;
- smoke attempts semantic/harness/test/DAP claim beyond policy;
- one target result reused for another target.

## Evidence and fan-in

- emit one target-specific receipt using #4837/#4843 when available;
- keep macOS/Windows receipt separate from Linux semantic/install evidence;
- feed #4346/#4350 and release-integrity subject-set validation;
- missing target smoke blocks only if that target is in the declared release matrix;
- a pass means packaged launch/identity/cleanup only.

## Acceptance

- [ ] Minimal process backend works on declared macOS and Windows release runners.
- [ ] Packaged candidate binaries—not workspace outputs—are launched.
- [ ] Artifact, target, version, and process identities match.
- [ ] Deadline and cleanup are proven.
- [ ] VSIX target mapping is proven where applicable.
- [ ] Receipts cannot be misread as semantic or debugger/test parity.
- [ ] #4350 can consume the results in the exact candidate rehearsal.

## Non-goals

- No macOS/Windows development CI.
- No upstream Perl harness or real-Perl oracle.
- No provider or framework semantic tests.
- No Test Explorer or DAP-session parity.
- No performance baseline.
- No broad process-supervisor feature parity with Linux.

